Most business owners think of website maintenance and SEO as two separate things. Maintenance keeps your site working; SEO helps it rank on Google. In practice, they're closely linked — and neglecting maintenance can undermine SEO work you've spent significant time and money building.

Here's how the connection works, and why maintaining your website is one of the most important things you can do to protect your search rankings.

## Google Factors Website Health into Rankings

Google's goal is to show users the best results for their search — and "best" includes more than just content relevance. A slow, insecure, or poorly maintained website delivers a poor experience to visitors, and Google factors this into how it ranks your site.

The specific technical factors that maintenance directly affects include:

### Page Speed

Site speed is a confirmed Google ranking factor. Google uses a set of metrics called Core Web Vitals — which measure loading speed, visual stability, and interactivity — to assess the experience your site provides. Slow pages rank lower.

As covered in [why websites slow down over time](/blog/why-websites-slow-down-over-time/), speed degrades gradually as software becomes outdated, images accumulate unoptimised, and databases grow. Regular maintenance prevents this degradation and keeps your speed scores healthy.

### Security

Google actively works to protect users from insecure websites. Sites that have been hacked, are serving malware, or don't have a valid SSL certificate are demoted in search results — or removed entirely. Google's Safe Browsing system flags compromised sites, and once flagged, recovering your rankings takes significant time even after the security issue is resolved.

A hacked website isn't just a security incident — it's an SEO disaster that can take months to recover from. Regular security monitoring, malware scanning, and prompt application of security updates protect your rankings by preventing the incident in the first place.

### Crawlability

Google's crawlers need to be able to reach and index your site. Broken pages (returning 404 errors), server errors, and redirect chains all make it harder for Google to crawl effectively. Maintenance includes checking for and resolving these issues — broken links, missing pages, and technical errors that accumulate unnoticed on unmaintained sites.

### Mobile Performance

Google uses mobile-first indexing, meaning it primarily evaluates the mobile version of your site when determining rankings. Plugin updates and software changes can sometimes affect how your site renders on mobile. Regular testing ensures your site continues to perform well on mobile devices after updates are applied.

## Maintenance and Your SEO Investment

If you're investing in [local SEO](/services/local-seo-google-ranking/) — whether through content creation, link building, or Google Business Profile management — you're building on a foundation. An unmaintained website is an unstable foundation.

SEO work can improve your rankings over time, but those improvements can be undone quickly by a security incident, a site speed problem, or a technical issue that prevents Google from crawling your pages. Maintenance protects the investment you've made in SEO by keeping the technical foundation solid.

## What Maintenance Actually Does for Your Rankings

Specifically, a well-maintained website:

- **Maintains fast load times** through regular performance monitoring and optimisation
- **Stays secure** through prompt updates, security monitoring, and malware scanning — preventing ranking penalties from security incidents
- **Stays crawlable** through broken link checks and technical error monitoring
- **Maintains mobile performance** through testing after updates are applied
- **Signals freshness to Google** through regular content updates and active maintenance

None of these things are SEO in the traditional sense — but they all affect how Google evaluates and ranks your site.

## Protecting What You've Built

If you've worked hard to build up your search rankings — whether through SEO investment, consistently publishing content, or simply having a well-built site — maintenance is what protects that position over time.

